---
// The code signing policy SignPath Foundation asks every project it signs for to publish, linked
// from every page's footer as "Code signing policy".
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Stub from '../components/Stub.astro';
---
<Base title="Code signing policy · Lemus" description="How Lemus's Windows releases are signed, who approves it, and what the plugins send over the network.">
<Stub heading="Code signing policy">
<p>Free code signing provided by <a href="https://signpath.io">SignPath.io</a>, certificate by <a href="https://signpath.org">SignPath Foundation</a>.</p>

<h2 class="h3">What is signed</h2>
<p>The Windows releases of Lemus plugins: each plugin's standalone app (.exe) and VST3. They are built by GitHub Actions from the public source at <a href="https://github.com/lemus-dev/lemus">github.com/lemus-dev/lemus</a>, from the release's version tag, and a person approves every signing request. The macOS installers are not signed through SignPath.</p>

<h2 class="h3">Team</h2>
<ul>
<li>Committers and reviewers: the <a href="https://github.com/orgs/lemus-dev/people">members of the lemus-dev organization</a> on GitHub. Changes from anyone else are reviewed by a member before they are merged.</li>
<li>Approvers: the owners of the lemus-dev organization.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="privacy" class="h3">Privacy</h2>
<p>The plugins collect nothing about you and send nothing anywhere you haven't asked them to, with one exception: once a day, while a plugin window is open, the update check reads one small file from lemusdev.org to see whether there is a newer version. The request carries no data about you. About › Check for updates once a day turns it off. The links in About open your browser.</p>
</Stub>
</Base>

